1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:10,400
Today, I'm chatting with Terence Tao, who needs no introduction. Terence, I want to begin by having you retell the story of how Kepler discovered the laws of

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:18,480
planetary motion because I think this will be a great jumping off point to talk about AI for math. I've always had an amateur interest in astronomy.

3
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:31,040
I've loved stories of how the early astronomers worked out the nature of the universe. Kepler was building on the work of Copernicus, who was himself building on the work of Aristarchus.

4
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:40,800
Copernicus very famously proposed the heliocentric model, that instead of the planets and the Sun going around the Earth, the Sun was at the center of the solar system and the

5
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:50,240
other planets were going around the Sun. Copernicus proposed that the orbits of the planets were perfect circles. His theory fit the observations

6
00:00:50,240 --> 00:01:04,160
that the Greeks, the Arabs, and the Indians had worked out over centuries. Kepler learned about these theories in his studies, and he made this observation that the

7
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:20,400
ratios of the size of the orbits that Copernicus predicted seemed to have some geometric meaning. He started proposing that if you take the orbit of the Earth and you enclose it in a cube,

8
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:30,640
the outer sphere that encloses the cube almost perfectly matched the orbit of Mars, and so forth. There were six planets known at the time and five gaps between them, and there were five perfect

9
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:38,880
Platonic solids: the cube, the tetrahedron, icosahedron, octahedron, and dodecahedron. So he had this theory, which he thought was absolutely beautiful,

10
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:50,320
that you could inscribe these Platonic solids between the spheres of the planets. It seemed to fit, and it seemed to him that God's design of the planets was matching this

11
00:01:50,320 --> 00:02:02,160
mathematical perfection of the Platonic solids. He needed data to confirm this theory. At the time, there was only one really high-quality dataset in existence.

12
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:12,560
Tycho Brahe, this very wealthy, eccentric Danish astronomer, had managed to convince the Danish government to fund this extremely expensive observatory.

13
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:24,480
In fact, it was an entire island where he had taken decades of observations of all the planets, like Mars and Jupiter, at least every night for which the weather was clear, with the naked eye.

14
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:32,240
He was the last of the naked-eye astronomers. He had all this data which Kepler could use to confirm his theory. Kepler started working with Tycho,

15
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:44,480
but Tycho was very jealous of the data. He only gave him little bits of it at a time. Kepler eventually just stole the data. He copied it and had to have a fight with

16
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:53,040
Brahe's descendants. He did get the data, and then he worked out, to his disappointment, that his beautiful theory didn't quite work.

17
00:02:53,040 --> 00:03:02,800
The data was off from his Platonic solid theory by 10% or something. He tried all kinds of fudges, moving the circles around, and it didn't quite work.

18
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:15,840
But he worked on this problem for years and years, and eventually, he figured out how to use the data to work out the actual orbits of the planets. That was an incredibly clever,

19
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:26,640
genius amount of data analysis. And then he worked out that the orbits were actually ellipses, not circles, which was shocking for him.

20
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:37,040
So he worked out the two laws of planetary motion: the ellipses, and also that equal areas sweep out equal times. Then ten years later, after

21
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:50,800
collecting a lot of data—the furthest planets like Saturn and Jupiter were the hardest for him to work out—he finally worked out this third law, that the time it takes for a

22
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:59,360
planet to complete its orbit was proportional to some power of the distance to the Sun. These are the three famous Kepler's laws of motion.

23
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:09,520
He had no explanation for them. It was all driven by experiment, and it took Newton a century later to give a theory that explained all three laws at once.

24
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:22,800
The take I want to try on you is that Kepler was a high-temperature LLM. Newton comes up with this explanation of why the three laws of planetary motion must be true.

25
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:29,760
Of course, the way that Kepler discovers the laws of planetary motion, or figures out the relative orbits of the different planets, is as you say a work of genius.

26
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:38,000
But through his career, he's just trying random relationships. In fact, in the book in which he writes down the third law of planetary motion,

27
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,720
it's an aside on The Harmonics of the World, which is just a book about how all these different planets have these different harmonies. And the reason there's so much famine

28
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:54,240
and misery on Earth is because the Earth is mi-fa-mi, that's the note of Earth. It's all this random astrology, but in there is the cube-square law,

29
00:04:54,240 --> 00:05:08,880
which tells you what relationship the period has to a planet's distance from the Sun. As you were detailing, if you add that to Newton's F=ma and the equation for centripetal

30
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:18,880
acceleration, you get the inverse-square law. And so Newton works that out. But the reason I think this is an interesting story is that I feel LLMs can do the kind of

31
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:27,600
thing of trying random relationships for twenty years, some of which make no sense, as long as there's a verifiable data bank like Brahe's dataset. "Ok, I'm going to try out random

32
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:36,960
things about musical notes, Platonic objects, or different geometries, I have this bias that there's some important thing about the geometry of these orbits." Then one thing works. As long

33
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:48,080
as you can verify it, these empirical regularities can then drive actual deep scientific progress. Traditionally, when we talk about the history of science, idea generation has

34
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:59,440
always been the prestige part of science. A scientific problem comes with many steps. You have to identify a problem, and then you have to identify a good, fruitful problem to work on.

35
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:07,280
Then you need to collect data, figure out a strategy to analyze the data, and make a hypothesis. At this point, you need to propose a

36
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:17,760
good hypothesis, and then you need to validate. Then you need to write things up and explain. There are a dozen different components. The ones we celebrate are these eureka

37
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:32,080
genius moments of idea generation. Kepler certainly had to cycle through many ideas, several of which didn't work. I bet there were many that he didn't even

38
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:41,440
publish at all because they just didn't fit. That's an important part of the process, trying all kinds of random things and seeing if they worked.

39
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:55,840
But as you say, it has to be matched by an equal amount of verification, otherwise it's slop. We celebrate Kepler, but we should also celebrate Brahe for his assiduous data

40
00:06:55,840 --> 00:07:09,120
collection, which was ten times more precise than any previous observation. That extra decimal point of accuracy was essential for Kepler to get his results.

41
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:22,960
He was using Euclidean geometry and the most advanced mathematics he could use at the time to match his models with the data. All aspects had to be in play: the data,

42
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:38,400
the theory, and the hypothesis generation. I'm not sure nowadays that hypothesis generation is the bottleneck anymore. Science has changed in the century since.

43
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:49,920
Classically, the two big paradigms for science were theory and experiment. Then in the 20th century, numerical simulation came along, so you can do

44
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:59,200
computer simulations to test theories. Finally, in the late 20th century, we had big data. We had the era of data analysis.

45
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:09,040
A lot of new progress is actually driven now by analyzing massive datasets first. You collect large datasets and then draw patterns from them to deduce thoughts.

46
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:17,440
This is a little bit different from how science used to work, where you make a few observations or have one out-of-the-blue idea, and then collect data to test your idea.

47
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:27,760
That's the classic scientific method. Now it's almost reversed. You collect big data first, and then you try to get hypotheses from it. Kepler was maybe one of the first early

48
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:40,240
data scientists, but even he didn't start with Tycho's dataset and then analyze it. He had some preconceived theories first. It seems like this is less and less the

49
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:54,880
way we make progress, just because the data is so much more massive and useful. Oh, interesting. I feel like the 20th-century science that you're describing actually very

50
00:08:54,880 --> 00:09:06,720
well describes what happened with Kepler. He did have these ideas—1595 and '96 is where he comes up with the polygons and then the Platonic objects theory—but they were wrong.

51
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:18,160
Then a few years later, he gets Brahe's data, and it's only after twenty years of trying random things that he gets this empirical regularity. It actually feels a bit closer to Brahe's data

52
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:28,000
being analogous to some massive data bank of simulations, and now that you've got the data, you can keep trying random things. If it wasn't for that, Kepler would be

53
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:39,600
out there just writing books about harmonics and Platonic objects, and there would be nothing to actually verify against. The data was extremely important. The

54
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:50,240
distinction I was trying to make was that traditionally, you make a hypothesis and then you test it against data. But now with machine learning,

55
00:09:50,240 --> 00:10:03,840
data analysis, and statistics, you can start with data and through statistics work out laws that were not present before. Kepler's third law is a little bit like this,

56
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:13,840
except that instead of having the thousand data points that Brahe had, Kepler had six data points. For every planet, he knew the length of the orbit and the distance to the Sun.

57
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:24,560
There were five or six data points, and he did what we would now call regression. He fit a curve to these six data points and got a square-cube law, which was amazing.

58
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:37,200
But he was quite lucky that these six data points gave him the right conclusion. That's not enough data to be really reliable. There was a later astronomer, Johann Bode,

59
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:48,720
who took the same data—the distances to the planets—and inspired by Kepler, he had a prediction that the distances to the planets formed a shifted geometric progression.

60
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:57,760
He also fit a curve, except there was one point missing. There was a big gap between Mars and Jupiter. His law predicted that there was a missing planet.

61
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:11,040
It was kind of a crank theory, except when Uranus was discovered by Herschel, the distance to Uranus fit exactly this pattern. Then Ceres was discovered in the asteroid belt,

62
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:20,480
and it also fit the pattern. People got really excited that Bode had discovered this amazing new law of nature. But then Neptune was discovered,

63
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:34,320
and it was way off. Basically it was just a numerical fluke. There were six data points. Maybe one reason why Kepler didn't highlight his third law as

64
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:45,040
much as the first two laws is that instinctively, even though he didn't have modern statistics, he kind of knew that with six data points, he had to be somewhat tentative with the conclusions.

65
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:58,560
To ask the question about the analogy more explicitly, does this analogy make sense if in the future we have smarter and smarter AIs? We'll have millions of them, and they can go out

66
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:08,560
and hunt for all these empirical irregularities. It sounds like you don't think the bottleneck in science is finding more things that are the equivalent of the third law of

67
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:18,240
planetary motion for each given field, so that later on somebody can say, "Oh, we need a way to explain this. Let's work out the math. Here's the inverse-square law of gravity."

68
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:27,920
I think AI has driven the cost of idea generation down to almost zero, in a very similar way to how the internet drove the cost of communication down to almost zero.

69
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:39,520
It’s an amazing thing, but it doesn't create abundance by itself. Now the bottleneck is different. We're now in a situation where suddenly people can generate thousands of

70
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:51,840
theories for a given scientific problem. Now we have to verify them, evaluate them. This is something which we have to change our structures of science to actually sort this

71
00:12:51,840 --> 00:13:07,680
out. Traditionally, we build walls. In the past, before we had AI slop, we had amateur scientists have their own theories of the universe, many of which were of very little value.

72
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:21,840
We built these peer review publication systems to filter out and try to isolate the high signal ideas to test. But now that we can generate these

73
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:34,560
possible explanations at massive scale, and some of them are good and a lot are terrible, human reviewers are already being overwhelmed. Many journals are reporting that AI-generated

74
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:46,480
submissions are just flooding their submissions. It's great that we can generate all kinds of things now with AI, but it means that the rest of the aspects of science have to catch up:

75
00:13:46,480 --> 00:14:02,880
verification, validation, and assessing what ideas actually move the subject forward and which ones are dead ends or red herrings. That's not something we know how to do at scale.

76
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:10,560
For each individual paper, we can have a debate among scientists and get to a consensus in a few years. But when we're generating a thousand of

77
00:14:10,560 --> 00:14:19,840
these every day, this doesn't work. There's this incredibly interesting question. If you have billions of AI scientists, not only how do you gauge

78
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:29,920
which ones are real progress, but how do you... This is actually a question that human science has had to face and we've solved somehow, and I’m actually not sure how we solved this.

79
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:41,200
Let's say in the 1940s, if you're at Bell Labs and there are these new technologies coming out. Pulse-code modulation, how do you transfer signals? How do you digitize signals?

80
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:50,800
How do you transfer them over analog wires? There are all these papers about the engineering constraints and the details, and then there's one which comes up with the idea of the bit, which has

81
00:14:50,800 --> 00:15:00,560
implications across many different fields. You need some system which can then look at that and say, "Okay, we need to apply this to probability. We need to apply this to computer science," et cetera.

82
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:10,960
In the future, the AIs are coming up with the next version of this unifying concept. How would you identify it among millions of papers that might actually constitute progress, but which

83
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:21,200
have much less in terms of general unifying ideas? A lot of it's the test of time. Many great ideas didn't actually get a great reception at the time they were first proposed.

84
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:29,360
It was only after some other scientists realized that they could take it further and apply them to their own... Deep learning itself was a

85
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:39,440
niche area of AI for a long time. The idea of getting answers entirely through training on data and not through first principles reasoning was very controversial,

86
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:50,400
and it just took a long time before it started bearing fruit. You mentioned the bit. There were other proposals for computer architectures than the zero-one that is universal today.

87
00:15:50,400 --> 00:16:03,760
I think there were trits, three-valued logic. In an alternate universe, maybe a different paradigm would have shown up. The transformer, for example, is the foundation of

88
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:13,200
all modern large language models, and it was the first deep learning architecture that really was sophisticated enough to capture language. But it didn't have to be that way.

89
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:25,600
There could've been some other architecture that was the first to do it and once that was adopted, it would become the standard. One reason why it's hard to

90
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:38,240
assess whether a given idea is going to be fruitful is that it depends on the future. It depends also on the culture and society, which ones get adopted, which ones don't.

91
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:48,000
The base ten numeral system in mathematics is extremely useful, much better than the Roman numeral system, for instance. But again, there's nothing special about ten.

92
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:57,040
It's a system that is useful for us because everyone else uses it. We've standardized it. We've built all our computers and our number representation

93
00:16:57,040 --> 00:17:12,480
systems around it, so we're stuck with it now. Some people occasionally push for other systems than decimal, but there's just too much inertia. It's not something where you can look at any given

94
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:25,040
scientific achievement purely in isolation and give it an objective grade without being aware of the context both in the past and the future. So it may never be something that you can

95
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:39,840
just reinforcement learn the same way that you can for much more localized problems. Often in the history of science when a new theory comes up that in retrospect we realize is correct,

96
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:50,560
it seems to make implications that either make no sense because they're wrong, and we realize later on why they're wrong, or they're correct but seem wildly implausible at the time.

97
00:17:50,560 --> 00:18:03,520
As you talked about, Aristarchus had heliocentrism in the third century BC. The ancient Athenians were like, "This can't be because if the earth is going around the sun,

98
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:13,440
we should see the relative position of the stars change as we're going around the sun, and the only way that wouldn't be the case is if they're so far away that you don't notice any parallax,"

99
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:19,360
which is actually the correct implication. But there's times when the implication is incorrect and we just need to graduate to a better level of understanding.

100
00:18:19,360 --> 00:18:31,680
Leibniz would chide Newton and disagree with Newton's theory of gravity on the basis that it implied action at a distance, and they didn't know the mechanism, and Newton himself

101
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:39,360
was sort of stunned that inertial mass and gravitational mass were the same quantity. All these things later were resolved by Einstein. But it was still progress. So

102
00:18:39,360 --> 00:18:49,920
the question for a system of peer review for AI would be: even if you can falsify a theory, how would you notice that it still constitutes progress relative to the thing before?

103
00:18:49,920 --> 00:19:00,640
Often, the ultimately correct theory initially is worse in many ways. Copernicus's theory of the planets was less accurate than Ptolemy's theory.

104
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:13,920
Geocentrism had been developed for a millennium by that point, and they had made many tweaks and increasingly complicated ad hoc fixes to make it more and more accurate.

105
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:21,200
Copernicus's theory was a lot simpler but much less accurate. It was only Kepler that made it more accurate than Ptolemy's theory.

106
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:34,000
Science is always a work in progress. When you only get part of the solution, it looks worse than a theory which is incorrect but somehow has been completed to the point

107
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:45,760
where it kind of answers all the questions. As you say, Newton's theory had big mysteries. They had the equivalence of mass and action at a distance, which

108
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:59,040
were only resolved with a very conceptually different approach centuries afterwards. Often progress has to be made not by adding more theories, but by deleting

109
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:10,720
some assumptions that you have in your mind. One reason why geocentrism held on for so long is we had this idea that objects naturally want to stay at rest.

110
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:19,200
This is the Aristotelian notion of physics, and so the idea that the Earth was moving… How come we weren't all falling over? Once you have Newton's laws of motion—an

111
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:30,480
object in motion remains in motion and so forth—then it makes sense. Conceptually, it's a very big leap to realize that the Earth is in motion.

112
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:42,880
It doesn't feel like it's in motion. The biggest advances, like Darwin's theory of evolution, is the idea that species are not static.

113
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:57,680
This is not obvious because you don't see evolution in your lifetime. Well, now we actually can, but it seems permanent and static.

114
00:20:57,680 --> 00:21:07,040
Right now we're going through a cognitive version of the Copernican revolution, where we used to think that human intelligence is the center of the universe, and now we're seeing that there are

115
00:21:07,040 --> 00:21:18,960
very different types of intelligence out there with very different strengths and weaknesses. Our assessment of which tasks require intelligence, which ones don't,

116
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:30,160
has to be reordered quite a bit. Trying to fit AI into our theories of scientific progress and what is hard and what is easy, we're struggling quite a lot.

117
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:39,600
We have to ask questions that we've never really had to ask before. Or maybe the philosophers had, but now we all have to deal with it. This brings up a topic I've been very curious about.

118
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:47,360
You mentioned Darwin's theory of evolution. There's this book, The Clockwork Universe by Edward Dolnick, which covers a lot of this era of history we're talking about.

119
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:57,680
He has this interesting observation in there. The Origin of Species was published in 1859. Principia Mathematica was published in 1687. So The Origin of Species comes out two centuries

120
00:21:57,680 --> 00:22:04,945
after Principia. Conceptually, it seems like Darwin's theory is simpler. There's a contemporaneous biologist to Darwin,

121
00:22:04,945 --> 00:22:12,080
Thomas Huxley, who reads The Origin of Species and he says, "How stupid not to have thought of that." Nobody ever says that about Principia, chiding themselves

122
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:20,400
for not having beaten Newton to gravity. So there's a question of why did it take longer? It seems like a big part of the reason is what you were saying.

123
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:27,520
The evidence for natural selection is overwhelming in a certain sense, but it's cumulative and retrospective, whereas Newton can just say, "Here are my equations.

124
00:22:27,520 --> 00:22:37,040
Let me see the moon's orbital period and its distance, and if it lines up, then we've made progress." Lucretius actually had this idea that species

125
00:22:37,040 --> 00:22:48,960
adapted to their environment in the first century BC but nobody really talks about it until Darwin because Lucretius couldn't run some experiment and force people to pay attention.

126
00:22:48,960 --> 00:23:00,000
I wonder if we'll in retrospect end up seeing much more progress in domains which have this kind of tight data loop where you can verify them quite easily, even though

127
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:09,360
they're conceptually much more difficult. I think one aspect of science is that it's not just creating a new theory and validating it, but communicating it to others.

128
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:22,560
Darwin was an amazing science communicator. He wrote in English, in natural language. I'm speaking like a— No Lean.

129
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:36,960
I have to get out of my technical mindset. He spoke in plain English, didn't use equations, and he synthesized a lot of disparate facts. Little pieces of evolution had been worked out in

130
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:49,920
the past, but he had this very compelling vision. Again, he was still missing things. He didn't know the mechanism for heredity, he didn't have DNA.

131
00:23:49,920 --> 00:24:02,720
But his writing style was persuasive, and that helped a lot. Newton wrote in Latin. He had invented entire new areas of mathematics just to explain what he was doing.

132
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:11,920
He was also from an era where scientists were much more secretive and competitive. Academia is still competitive, but it was even worse back in Newton's day.

133
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:23,040
He held back some of his best insights because he didn't want his rivals to get any advantage. He was also a somewhat unpleasant person from what I gather.

134
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:39,120
It was only a couple of decades after Newton when other scientists explained his work in much simpler terms that they became widespread. The art of exposition and making a case and

135
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:50,320
creating a narrative is also a very important part of science. If you have the data, it helps, but people need to be convinced, otherwise they will not push

136
00:24:50,320 --> 00:25:01,440
it further or take the initial investment to learn your theory and really explore it. That's another thing which is really hard to reinforcement learn on.

137
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:08,720
How can you score how persuasive you are? Well, there are entire marketing departments trying to do this. Maybe it's good that AI is not

138
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:23,520
yet optimized to be persuasive. There's a social aspect to science. Even though we pride ourselves on having an objective side to it, where there's data and

139
00:25:23,520 --> 00:25:41,520
experiment and validation, we still have to tell stories and convince our fellow scientists. That's a soft, squishy thing. It's a combination of data and painting a narrative,

140
00:25:41,520 --> 00:25:51,200
and it's a narrative of gaps. Even with Darwin, as I said, there were pieces of his theory he could not explain. But he could still make a case that in the future,

141
00:25:51,200 --> 00:26:01,280
people would find transitional forms, that they would find the mechanism of inheritance, and they did. I don't know how you can quantify that

142
00:26:01,280 --> 00:26:10,880
in such a precise way that you can start doing reinforcement learning. Maybe that will be forever the human side of science.

143
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:22,560
One takeaway I had from reading and watching your stuff on the cosmic distance ladder… By the way, I highly recommend people watch your series with 3Blue1Brown on the cosmic distance ladder.

144
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:33,600
