00:53:32,880 --> 00:53:44,320
advance our understanding of mathematics? Or could it just be assembly code gobbledygook? We don't know. Some problems have been basically solved by pure brute force.

289
00:53:44,320 --> 00:53:56,400
The four color theorem is a famous example. We have still not found a conceptually elegant proof of this theorem, and maybe we never will. Some problems may only be solvable by splitting

290
00:53:56,400 --> 00:54:08,560
into an enormous number of cases and doing brute force, uninsightful computer analysis on each case. Part of the reason we prize problems like

291
00:54:08,560 --> 00:54:18,960
the Riemann hypothesis is that we're pretty sure a new type of mathematics has to be created, or a new connection between two previously unconnected areas of mathematics

292
00:54:18,960 --> 00:54:30,400
has to be discovered to make this work. We don't even know what the shape of the solution is, but it doesn't feel like a problem that will be solved just by exhaustively checking cases.

293
00:54:30,400 --> 00:54:42,320
Or it could be false actually. Okay, there is an unlikely scenario that the hypothesis is false, and you can just compute a zero off the line,

294
00:54:42,320 --> 00:54:59,200
and a massive computer calculation verifies it. That would be very disappointing. I do feel that fully autonomous, one-shot approaches are not the right approach for these problems.

295
00:54:59,200 --> 00:55:14,560
You'll get a lot more mileage out of the interplay of humans collaborating with these tools. I can see one of these problems being solved by smart humans

296
00:55:14,560 --> 00:55:24,480
assisted by extremely powerful AI tools. But the exact dynamic may be very different from what we envision right now. It could be a collaboration of

297
00:55:24,480 --> 00:55:41,920
a type that just doesn't exist yet. There may be a way to generate a million variants of the Riemann zeta function and do AI-assisted data analysis to discover some pattern connecting

298
00:55:41,920 --> 00:55:51,200
them that we didn't know about before. This lets you transform the problem into a different area of mathematics. There could be all kinds of scenarios.

299
00:55:51,200 --> 00:56:04,720
Suppose the AI figures it out, and latent in the Lean is some brand-new construction which, if we realized its significance, we would be able to apply in all these different situations.

300
00:56:04,720 --> 00:56:17,040
How would we even recognize it? Again, a very naive question, but if you come up with the equivalent of Descartes' idea that you can have a coordinate system unifying algebra

301
00:56:17,040 --> 00:56:26,160
and geometry, in Lean code it would just look like R→R, and it wouldn't look that significant. I'm sure there are other constructions which have this kind of property.

302
00:56:26,160 --> 00:56:39,040
The beauty of formalizing a proof in something like Lean is that you can take any piece of it and study it atomically. When I read a paper which solves some

303
00:56:39,040 --> 00:56:48,480
difficult problem, there's often a big sequence of lemmas and theorems. Ideally, the author will talk their way through what's important and what's not.

304
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:59,360
But sometimes they don't reveal what steps were the important ones and which ones were just boilerplate, standard steps. You can study each lemma in isolation.

305
00:56:59,360 --> 00:57:06,320
Some of them I can see look fairly standard and resemble something I'm familiar with. I'm pretty sure there's nothing interesting going on there.

306
00:57:06,320 --> 00:57:21,280
But this other lemma, that's something I haven't seen before, and I can see why having this result would really help prove the main result. You can assess whether a step is really key

307
00:57:21,280 --> 00:57:29,520
to your argument or not, and Lean really facilitates that. The individual steps are identified really precisely.

308
00:57:29,520 --> 00:57:41,120
I think in the future, there will be entire professions of mathematicians who might take a giant Lean-generated proof and do some ablation on it, trying to remove parts of

309
00:57:41,120 --> 00:57:52,160
it and find more elegant ways. They might get other AIs to do some reinforcement learning to make the proof more elegant, and maybe other AIs will grade

310
00:57:52,160 --> 00:58:01,920
whether this proof looks better or not. One thing that will change quite a bit in the near future is how we write papers. Until recently, writing papers was the most

311
00:58:01,920 --> 00:58:14,560
time-consuming and expensive part of the job. So you did it very rarely. You only wrote up your results once all the other parts of your argument were checked out, because

312
00:58:14,560 --> 00:58:24,960
rewriting and refactoring was just a total pain. That's become a lot easier now with modern AI tools. You don't have to have just one version of your paper. Once you have one,

313
00:58:24,960 --> 00:58:35,360
people can generate hundreds more. One giant messy Lean proof may not be very meaningful or understandable on its own, but other people can

314
00:58:35,360 --> 00:58:47,840
refactor it and do all kinds of things with it. We've seen this with the Erdős problem website. An AI will generate a proof, and here are 3,000 lines of code that verify the proof.

315
00:58:47,840 --> 00:59:00,480
Then people got other AIs to summarize the proof, and people write their own proofs. There's actually post-processing. Once you have one proof, we have a lot of

316
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:12,000
tools now to deconstruct and interpret it. It's a very nascent area of mathematics, but I'm not as worried about it. Some people are concerned about what happens if

317
00:59:12,000 --> 00:59:20,640
the Riemann hypothesis is proven with a completely incomprehensible proof. I think once you have the artifact of a proof, we can do a lot of analysis on it.

318
00:59:20,640 --> 00:59:28,960
You posted recently that it would be helpful to have a formal or semi-formal language for mathematical strategies as opposed to just mathematical proofs,

319
00:59:28,960 --> 00:59:37,840
which is what Lean specializes in. I would love to learn more about what that would involve or look like. We don't really know. We've been very

320
00:59:37,840 --> 00:59:47,920
lucky in mathematics that we have worked out the laws of logic and mathematics, but this is a fairly recent accomplishment. It was started by Euclid two millennia ago,

321
00:59:47,920 --> 01:00:00,400
but only in the early 20th century did we finally list out the axioms of mathematics, the standard axioms of what we call ZFC, the axioms of first-order logic, and what a proof is.

322
01:00:00,400 --> 01:00:14,080
This we've managed to automate and have a formal language for. There could be some way to assess plausibility. You have a conjecture that something is true,

323
01:00:14,080 --> 01:00:24,560
you test a few examples, and it works out. How does this increase your confidence that the conjecture is true? We have a few sort of mathematical ways to model

324
01:00:24,560 --> 01:00:44,880
this, like Bayesian probability, for example. But you often have to set certain base assumptions, and there's a lot of subjectivity still in these tasks.

325
01:00:44,880 --> 01:00:59,840
This is more of a wish than a plan to develop these languages, but just seeing how successful having a formal framework in place, like Lean, has made deductive proofs so much easier to automate

326
01:00:59,840 --> 01:01:16,880
and train AI on… The bottleneck for using AI to create strategies and make conjectures is we have to rely on human experts and the test of time to validate whether something is plausible or not.

327
01:01:16,880 --> 01:01:32,240
If there was some semi-formal framework where this could be done semi-automatically in a way that isn't easily hackable... It's really important with these formal

328
01:01:32,240 --> 01:01:44,960
proof assistants that there are no backdoors or exploits you can use to somehow get your certified proof without actually proving it, because reinforcement learning is

329
01:01:44,960 --> 01:02:01,280
just so good at finding these backdoors. If there's some framework that mimics how scientists talk to each other in a semi-formal way, using data and argument,

330
01:02:01,280 --> 01:02:18,160
but also constructing narratives... There's some subjective aspect of science that we don't know how to capture in a way that we can insert AI into it in any useful way.

331
01:02:18,160 --> 01:02:35,440
This is a future problem. There are research efforts to try to create automated conjectures, and maybe there are ways to benchmark these and simulate this, but it's all very new science.

332
01:02:35,440 --> 01:02:56,240
Can you help me get some intuition? I have two sub-questions. One, it would be very helpful to have a specific example of what something like this would look like, the way scientists

333
01:02:56,240 --> 01:03:10,160
communicate that we can't formalize yet. Two, it seems almost definitionally paradoxical to say you're building up some narrative or natural language explanation and then also having

334
01:03:10,160 --> 01:03:20,400
something which you could have formalized. I'm sure there's some intuition behind where that overlap is, and I'd love to understand that better.

335
01:03:20,400 --> 01:03:31,760
An example of a conjecture: Gauss was interested in the prime numbers and created one of the first mathematical datasets. He just computed the first 100,000 prime numbers

336
01:03:31,760 --> 01:03:41,120
or so, hoping to find patterns. He did find a pattern, but maybe not the pattern he was expecting. He found a statistical pattern in the primes

337
01:03:41,120 --> 01:03:54,080
that if you count how many primes there are up to 100, 1,000, one million, and so forth, they get sparser and sparser, but the drop-off in the density was inversely proportional to

338
01:03:54,080 --> 01:04:05,120
the natural logarithm of the range of numbers. So he conjectured what we now call the prime number theorem: the number of primes up to X is X divided by the natural log of X.

339
01:04:05,120 --> 01:04:19,520
He had no way to prove this. It was data-driven. This was a conjecture. It was revolutionary for its time because it was maybe the first really important conjecture

340
01:04:19,520 --> 01:04:27,600
of math that was statistical in nature. Normally you're talking about a pattern, like maybe the spacing between the primes has a certain regularity.

341
01:04:27,600 --> 01:04:42,640
But this didn't tell you exactly how many primes there were in any given range. It just gave you an approximation that got better and better as you went further and further out.

342
01:04:42,640 --> 01:04:51,040
It started the field of what we call analytic number theory. It was the first in many conjectures like this, many of which got proved,

343
01:04:51,040 --> 01:05:03,360
which started consolidating the idea that the prime numbers didn't really have a pattern, that they behaved like random sets of numbers with a certain density.

344
01:05:03,360 --> 01:05:14,160
They had some patterns, like they're almost all odd. They're also not actually random, they're what's called pseudo-random. There's no random number generation involved in creating the prime numbers.

345
01:05:14,160 --> 01:05:26,000
But over time, it became more and more productive to think of the primes as if they were just generated by some god rolling dice all the time and creating this random set.

346
01:05:26,000 --> 01:05:32,640
This allowed us to make all these other predictions. There's a still-open conjecture in number theory called the twin prime conjecture, that

347
01:05:32,640 --> 01:05:41,040
there should be infinitely many pairs of primes that are twins just two apart, like 11 and 13. We can't prove that, and there are good reasons why we can't prove it.

348
01:05:41,040 --> 01:05:51,680
But because of this statistical random model of the primes, we are absolutely convinced it's true. We know that if the primes were generated by flipping coins, we would just—by random chance

349
01:05:51,680 --> 01:06:02,320
like infinite monkeys at a typewriter—see twin primes appear over and over again. We have over time developed this very accurate conceptual model of what the primes should behave

350
01:06:02,320 --> 01:06:12,400
like based on statistics and probability. It's mostly heuristic and non-rigorous, but extremely accurate. The few times when we actually

351
01:06:12,400 --> 01:06:21,600
can prove things about the primes, it has matched up with the predictions of what we call the random model of the primes. We have this conjectural concept

352
01:06:21,600 --> 01:06:29,680
framework for understanding the primes that everyone believes in. It's the same reason why we believe the Riemann hypothesis is true, and why we

353
01:06:29,680 --> 01:06:40,800
believe that cryptography based on the primes is mathematically secure. It's all part of this belief. In fact, one reason why we care

354
01:06:40,800 --> 01:06:49,920
about the Riemann hypothesis is that if the Riemann hypothesis failed, if we knew it was false, it would be a serious blow to this model.

355
01:06:49,920 --> 01:06:58,080
It would mean there's a secret pattern to the primes that we were not aware of. I think we would very rapidly abandon any cryptography based on the primes,

356
01:06:58,080 --> 01:07:07,680
because if there was one pattern that we didn't know about, there are probably more, and these patterns can lead to exploits in crypto. It would be a big shock.

357
01:07:07,680 --> 01:07:20,320
So we really want to make sure that doesn't happen. We've been convinced of things like the Riemann hypothesis over time.

358
01:07:20,320 --> 01:07:30,000
Some of it is experimental evidence, and some is that the few times we've been able to make theoretical results, they've always aligned. It is possible that the consensus is wrong and

359
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:40,480
we've all just missed something very basic. There have been paradigm shifts in the past in scientific history. But we don't really have a way of

360
01:07:40,480 --> 01:07:53,600
measuring this, partly because we don't have enough data on how math or science develops. We have one timeline of history, and we have maybe 100 stories of turning points in history.

361
01:07:53,600 --> 01:08:04,720
If we had access to a million alien civilizations, each with a different development of history and science in different orders, then maybe we'd actually have a decent

362
01:08:04,720 --> 01:08:18,319
shot at understanding how we measure what progress is and what is a good strategy. We could maybe start formalizing it and actually having a framework.

363
01:08:18,319 --> 01:08:30,240
Maybe what we need to do is start creating lots of mini-universes or simulations of AI solving very basic problems in arithmetic or whatever, but coming up with their

